+# Lesson: Money in `src/billing` is integer cents, never floats
+
+## The rule
+
+In `src/billing`, store and calculate all money as **integer cents** (`int`).
+Do not use floats for money, and do not use `round()` on a float to "fix" a total.
+
+## What went wrong
+
+In the March invoice run, a float total came out as `10.299999…`. Rounding it
+did not give the right answer: `npm run invoices` showed **10.29** where the
+correct amount was **10.30**.
+
+Floats are binary, so they can't hold most decimal amounts exactly. Small
+errors pile up when you add amounts together. Rounding at the end can't recover
+the right value, because the value is already wrong before you round it.
+
+## What to do instead
+
+- Keep prices, line items and totals as integer cents (`1030`, not `10.30`).
+- Add up the cents as integers. Integer addition is exact.
+- Convert to dollars only when you display the amount, using integer math.
+  For example: `f"{cents // 100}.{cents % 100:02d}"`.
+- If a calculation really needs fractions (tax rates, proration, currency
+  conversion), use `decimal.Decimal` with a rounding mode you choose on purpose,
+  then convert the result back to integer cents.
+
+```python
+# Wrong: float money, rounded at the end
+def total(items):
+    return round(sum(i["price"] for i in items), 2)
+
+# Right: integer cents all the way through
+def total_cents(items):
+    return sum(i["price_cents"] for i in items)
+```
+
+## Where this applies now
+
+`src/billing/total.py` still sums float `price` values and calls
+`round(..., 2)`. That is the pattern this lesson warns against. It should move
+to integer cents, along with any callers and stored data that pass float prices.
+
+## Review checklist for billing changes
+
+- [ ] No `float` money values or float literals like `10.30` for amounts
+- [ ] No `round()` on money
+- [ ] Field names make the unit clear (`*_cents`)
+- [ ] Conversion to a display string happens only at the output edge
+- [ ] Tests include amounts that floats get wrong (for example `0.10 + 0.20`, and totals like 10.30)
